      "text": "2 November saw the world's first major internet crisis unfold when the **Morris worm** was released into the nascent ARPANET from a computer terminal at MIT. Programmed by 23-year-old Cornell University graduate student Robert Tappan Morris to gauge the size of the network, a critical flaw in the worm's propagation code caused it to replicate uncontrollably, bringing down an estimated 6,000 Unix machines—roughly 10% of the entire connected internet. The incident caused millions in downtime and led to the creation of the world's first Computer Emergency Response Team (CERT).",
